from tnsoc import *
S(9,6,"Asha visited a fort and saw old temples nearby. \"Who built these?\" she asked.",
 ["Medieval India saw many kingdoms. In the north, the Delhi Sultanate was ruled by dynasties such as the Slaves, Khaljis and Tughlaqs. In the south, the Cholas, Pandyas and later Vijayanagar ruled, and the Cholas built the Brihadeeswarar temple at Thanjavur.",
  "The Mughal empire was founded by Babur in 1526, and Akbar, Shah Jahan and others ruled. Land revenue and an administrative system supported the state. The Bhakti and Sufi movements promoted unity and devotion.",
  "Art and architecture flourished, with forts, mosques, temples and the Taj Mahal. Trade connected India to many lands."],
 ["Delhi Sultanate","Cholas","Vijayanagar","Mughals","Bhakti","Sufi"],
 ("Why are the Chola temples important?",["They show great architecture.","They were centres of art and life.","Brihadeeswarar is a world heritage site."]),
 ("Babur founded the Delhi Sultanate.",["He founded the Mughal empire in 1526.","The Sultanate began earlier."]),
 ["Delhi Sultanate in the north.","Cholas and Vijayanagar in the south.","Mughal empire founded in 1526.","Bhakti and Sufi spread unity."],
 ["m|The Mughal empire was founded by|Babur|Akbar|Ashoka|In 1526.","t|The Cholas built the Thanjavur temple.|T|Yes.","m|Vijayanagar was in|south India|north India|Bengal|Deccan south.","t|Bhakti stressed devotion.|T|Yes.","m|The Taj Mahal was built under|Shah Jahan|Babur|Akbar|Mughal monument.","s|Which empire began in 1526?|mughal|Babur."])
S(9,7,"Raju asked how sailors found their way to new lands. \"Compass and maps,\" said the teacher.",
 ["The modern age began with the Renaissance, a rebirth of art and learning in Europe from the 14th century. Artists like Leonardo da Vinci and Michelangelo and thinkers like Galileo changed how people saw the world.",
  "Printing with movable type by Gutenberg spread knowledge. The Reformation, led by Martin Luther, questioned the Church. The Age of Discovery sent Columbus to America and Vasco da Gama to India in 1498.",
  "The new sea routes opened trade and led to colonies. Voyages brought wealth but also hardship for native peoples."],
 ["Renaissance","Reformation","Gutenberg","Age of Discovery","Vasco da Gama","colony"],
 ("Why did Europeans search for sea routes?",["To reach the spice trade of Asia.","Land routes were hard.","They hoped for wealth."]),
 ("Vasco da Gama reached America.",["He reached India in 1498.","Columbus reached America."]),
 ["Renaissance means rebirth.","Printing spread ideas.","Reformation questioned the Church.","Sea routes led to colonies."],
 ["m|The Renaissance began in|Europe|Africa|Australia|Rebirth of learning.","t|Gutenberg developed printing.|T|Yes.","m|Vasco da Gama reached India in|1498|1492|1600|By sea.","t|Leonardo da Vinci was a scientist-artist.|T|Yes.","m|Martin Luther led the|Reformation|Renaissance|Crusade|Church reform.","s|Who reached America in 1492?|columbus|Columbus."])
S(9,8,"Meena read the words liberty, equality, fraternity. \"Where did they come from?\" she asked.",
 ["The age of revolutions changed the world. The American Revolution (1776) freed thirteen colonies from Britain, and the Declaration of Independence spoke of equal rights. The French Revolution (1789) began against the king's rule and high taxes and declared liberty, equality and fraternity.",
  "Napoleon rose to power in France and spread revolutionary ideas, but he was defeated at Waterloo in 1815. The Latin American countries won independence from Spain and Portugal under leaders such as Simon Bolivar.",
  "These revolutions inspired people everywhere to demand rights and ended old monarchies in many places."],
 ["revolution","Declaration of Independence","liberty, equality, fraternity","Napoleon","Waterloo","Bolivar"],
 ("What did the French Revolution give the world?",["The idea of liberty, equality and fraternity.","Rights of citizens.","It inspired other movements."]),
 ("The American Revolution happened in 1789.",["It was in 1776.","1789 was the French Revolution."]),
 ["American Revolution: 1776.","French Revolution: 1789.","Napoleon defeated at Waterloo.","Revolutions spread rights."],
 ["m|The French Revolution began in|1789|1776|1815|Bastille.","t|The motto was liberty, equality and fraternity.|T|Yes.","m|Napoleon was defeated at|Waterloo|Paris|Delhi|In 1815.","t|The American colonies won freedom from Britain.|T|Yes.","m|Latin American leader|Bolivar|Luther|Gandhi|Simon Bolivar.","s|In which year did the American Revolution declare independence?|1776|Declaration."])
S(9,9,"Asha looked at a machine in a cloth mill. \"Machines changed everything,\" said her teacher.",
 ["The Industrial Revolution began in Britain in the 18th century when hand production gave way to machines and factories. Inventions included the spinning jenny, the power loom and James Watt's steam engine.",
  "Coal and iron fuelled industry, railways and steamships moved goods, and towns grew into cities. Factories needed workers, and many worked long hours in poor conditions, including children.",
  "Industrial growth increased the demand for raw materials and markets, and led to colonies. It also brought new classes, labour movements and laws to protect workers."],
 ["Industrial Revolution","steam engine","factory","railways","urbanisation","labour"],
 ("Why did the revolution begin in Britain?",["It had coal and iron.","It had colonies for raw materials.","It had capital and inventors."]),
 ("The steam engine was invented by Gutenberg.",["It was improved by James Watt.","Gutenberg invented printing."]),
 ["Machines replaced hand work.","Steam engine and railways.","Cities grew.","Workers faced hard conditions."],
 ["m|The Industrial Revolution began in|Britain|India|China|18th century.","t|The power loom was an invention.|T|Yes.","m|The steam engine was improved by|James Watt|Newton|Edison|Key invention.","t|Factories reduced the size of towns.|F|Towns grew.","m|Industry needed|raw materials and markets|only farms|no coal|Led to colonies.","s|Which fuel powered early factories?|coal|And steam."])
S(9,10,"Raju found Africa on the world map, divided into many straight-edged countries. \"Why so straight?\" he asked.",
 ["Colonialism means one country ruling and using another. European powers like Britain, France, Portugal, Spain and the Netherlands built colonies in Asia and Africa to get raw materials and markets. The Scramble for Africa saw them divide the continent at the Berlin Conference of 1884.",
  "In Asia, Britain ruled India, the Dutch ruled Indonesia, and the French ruled Indo-China. Colonial rule drained wealth, changed farming and harmed local industries, but it also brought railways and new ideas.",
  "People resisted through revolts and later national movements, and most colonies became free in the 20th century."],
 ["colonialism","raw materials","Scramble for Africa","Berlin Conference","resistance","independence"],
 ("Why did Europeans want colonies?",["For raw materials.","For markets to sell goods.","For power and prestige."]),
 ("The Berlin Conference gave Africa its freedom.",["It divided Africa among Europeans.","Freedom came in the 20th century."]),
 ["Colonialism is rule over another land.","Africa was divided in 1884.","Britain ruled India.","Colonies later became free."],
 ["m|Colonies gave Europe|raw materials and markets|nothing|only gold|Economic reasons.","t|Britain ruled India.|T|Yes.","m|The Scramble for Africa was among|European powers|Asian kings|Africans|They divided it.","t|Colonial rule helped local industries grow.|F|It harmed many.","m|The Dutch ruled|Indonesia|India|Egypt|East Indies.","s|In which continent was the Berlin Conference's division made?|africa|Africa."])
